[4] The yeast in hot cross buns can ferment its various sugars enough for ‘alcohol’ to register on a passive roadside alcometer test.

[5] However, this does not mean it would put a driver over the limit, following further breath analysis procedures.

[6] Last week, a Pilbara truck driver, Heather Jones, blew 0.018 on an alcometer after a mouthful of a such a bun.

[7] Her video went viral on social media, warning other truck drivers about her results. For a professional driver, such as a truck driver, the legal blood alcohol limit is 0.02.

[8] Inspector Alison Brennan of Hume Police District confirmed that foods and substances other than alcoholic drink could register on the passive roadside breath test device.

[9] She explained how such a situation could occur.

[10] “In training to become a breath analysis operator, we test all sorts of stuff,” she said, “from mouth wash to garlic to all sorts of things, to see if they will register.

[11] “There are foods and substances that can give a reading on a passive roadside test, but this is exactly why we have these procedures so there can be no doubt,” she said.

[12] The procedure is a passive test using the alcometer, where a driver speaks into a hand-held device.

[13] If the alcometer indicates that alcohol is present, the police then attach a tube to the hand-held device to get a sample of air from the driver’s lungs, not their mouth.

[14] At that point, the ferment of a hot cross bun, for example, should not register an alcoholic reading.

[15] However, if alcohol was present and over the legal limit, the police would then detain the driver and attend the nearest police station.

[16] At the police station, a 15-minute observation period should ensure no residual mouth alcohol in a reading.

[17] The driver would then be tested on the breath analysis machine, Inspector Brennan said, adding it had “a margin of error so negligible as to be irrefutable”.

[18] Other positive readings in a passive roadside test could be caused by alcohol-based hand wash, an inebriated passenger breathing heavily, strong perfume, or liquor-flavoured desserts.

[19] But as with the hot cross bun, these should not register beyond the initial passive breath test on the roadside.

[20] Brewer Andy Orrell, of Hairyman Brewery and 30 years’ experience, explained the hot cross bun finding.

[21] He said it was likely the baking yeast had the potential to consume the sugars, which had started fermentation.

[22] The yeast could have fermented the sugars in the milled flour, the fruit, and the sugar glaze, he said.

[23] “The sugars have been fermented by the yeast, just like beer would have,” he said.